INT. NIGHT TRAIN SLEEPER COMPARTMENT - NIGHT

Rhythmic clatter. A reading lamp swings on its hook. Rain streaks the window. Two bunks, one folded away.

WENDELL ODUYA-FENWICK, 40s, sits by the window in shirtsleeves, tie loosened. His wife CLEMENTINE ACHEBE-LINDQVIST, 40s, on the bunk, braids her hair without looking.

Her sister LAVINIA BELLINI-OKORO, 30s, stands in the doorway with a suitcase. And a conductor, ANATOLE KOWALCZYK-ADEYEMI, 50s, hovers in the corridor, punching tickets.

He glances at the others. She doesn't glance back. They have not spoken for a long time.

LAVINIA
Is there room?

CLEMENTINE
There's always room.

LAVINIA
For a suitcase?

WENDELL
For a suitcase, yes.

She enters. He shifts. Their knees brush. He pulls his away.

ANATOLE
Tickets, please.

He punches two. Hesitates at the third. Clementine hands it over without a word. He nods, moves on.

The door slides shut. The train rocks. Lavinia sits on the lower bunk, opposite Clementine.

LAVINIA
You look tired.

CLEMENTINE
You look thin.

LAVINIA
I've been travelling.

CLEMENTINE
So have we.

WENDELL
Where to?

LAVINIA
Anywhere with a sea.

CLEMENTINE
There's a sea at the end of this line.

LAVINIA
That's what I heard.

WENDELL
Who told you?

LAVINIA
A man at the station. He said the sea turns green in September.

CLEMENTINE
It does.

LAVINIA
Have you seen it?

CLEMENTINE
Once. A long time ago.

WENDELL
With me.

CLEMENTINE
With you.

A pause. The train sways. Lavinia looks at her hands. Wendell looks at the rain. Clementine finishes the braid and ties it off.

LAVINIA
I should have written.

CLEMENTINE
You should have.

LAVINIA
I'm sorry.

CLEMENTINE
So am I.

WENDELL
Shall I open a window?

LAVINIA
It's raining.

WENDELL
It's close in here.

CLEMENTINE
Leave it.

WENDELL
All right.

LAVINIA
I met someone.

CLEMENTINE
Good.

LAVINIA
It didn't end well.

CLEMENTINE
They rarely do.

WENDELL
Clementine.

CLEMENTINE
What? It's true.

LAVINIA
No, she's right. They don't.

WENDELL
Do you want tea?

LAVINIA
Please.

He rises. Opens the door. Disappears into the corridor. The two sisters sit across from each other, alone, the rain tapping the glass.

CLEMENTINE
He's changed.

LAVINIA
Has he?

CLEMENTINE
He lets me braid my own hair.

They share a smile. Small. Then bigger. Lavinia wipes her eye with the back of her hand.

LAVINIA
I missed you.

CLEMENTINE
I know.

She reaches across, pulls her sister close, and holds her. Over Lavinia's shoulder she watches the door.

Wendell returns with three cups. He stops in the doorway, sees them. Sets the tray down gently on the bunk and quietly steps back out.
